id: core/node
title: "core node"
kind: mental-model-node
status: active-mixed
updated: 2026-09-01

purpose: >
  Hold the public core concepts for understanding and using .yawn.

holds:
  - what a yawn is
  - orientation
  - co-orientation loop
  - memory update
  - movement frame
  - movement loop
  - lacuna
  - agency
  - observer and body
  - proof and boundary
  - folder-node protocol
  - state substrate and event reduction
  - optional target conditions and typed lacunae
  - state transition intent and result
  - motivation and purpose
  - world, field, and arena boundaries
  - Yawn holarchy and typed relations
  - asynchronous turns and causal time
  - arena discovery and structural routing
  - objective holons and Yawn.bot activation
  - projection, expression-provision, View, and aperture boundaries
  - adaptive inquiry and move selection with replay receipts
  - source-bound Yawnbots and the recursive Question-Position-Expression-Move-Evaluation grammar

receives:
  - readme.yawn
  - shape primitives
  - scientific frame
  - bridge-layer core files

produces:
  - public ontology terms
  - safe movement rules
  - reusable mental models

questions:
  - what is the smallest true concept here
  - how does movement stay bounded
  - what does proof protect
  - what arena is the smallest adequate cut
  - when should a signal attach, nest, link, merge, split, or remain held
  - when should an objective receive its own Yawn and sleeping bot
  - which orientation question or explicit hold is useful now
  - what Question is this Yawnbot trying to resolve
  - which attributed Position currently answers it
  - how may that Position be provisioned into an interaction without changing it
  - what actually moved, what consequence was observed, and who evaluated it according to what
  - which judgment should open a new question rather than silently become proof

